   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the adoption of the amendment law (Law No. 191/2020), the State Labour Inspectorate (SLI) have (re-)recruited 84 labour inspectors (the plan is to fill 105 vacancies in total). The State Labour Inspectorate made is compulsary for newly recruited inspectors to complete the ILO course to equip them with basic knowledge and skills on principles, policies, strategies, and practical methods for inspection visits.

In addition, the procedures and the organizational structure of the inspectorate are being rebuilt. An IT platform that will serve as a key tool for efficient conduct of labour inspections has been under development. 

Government Decision to facilitate the recruitment of the staff of the State Labour Inspectorate and Government Decision allowing the IT system of the State Labour Inspectorate to exchange data with other government IT platform are expected to be adopted by the end of 2021. With RBSA support, the ILO trained labour and OSH inspectors to better enforce the OSH legislation and tackle undeclared work, including the provision of comprehensive training modules &quot;Building modern and effective labour inspection systems&quot;.

An E-learning platform and Knowledge platform as resources for the State Labour Inspectorate, including a feasibility study of the labour inspection management system is being conducted and will be finalized by the end of 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
